<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1935 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Faccetta nera</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An Italian marching song of 1935 from Fascist Italy's invasion of Ethiopia, which it presented as a war to free slaves.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Renato Micheli</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Mario Ruccione</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Fascist Italy during the invasion and occupation of Ethiopia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Rome, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>April 1935</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Renato Micheli, music by Mario Ruccione, 1935)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faccetta-nera">Faccetta nera</a>&quot; (&quot;Little black face&quot;) was written by the Roman poet Renato Micheli, with music by Mario Ruccione, in April 1935, as the Italian army prepared to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Fascist propaganda was then publishing stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Ethiopia, and it presented the coming invasion not as a war of conquest but as a war of liberation to abolish slavery; these stories served in part to justify the conquest. Micheli wrote the song in Romanesco, the dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, after reading them, meaning to enter it in the 1935 festival of Roman song. It is sung by a Blackshirt soldier to a young enslaved Ethiopian girl, and it praised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s colonial &quot;civilising mission&quot; by alluding to madamato, the concubinage of Italian men with Ethiopian women.</p><p>Nothing came of the festival, but once Ruccione had set it the singer Carlo Buti performed it at the Teatro Capranica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and at the Quattro Fontane <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> Anna Fougez's company staged it with a young Black woman in chains, freed by Fougez dressed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and made to put on a black shirt. Taken into many revues, the song was hugely popular from the first days of the invasion, especially among the troops leaving for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. The Italian article stresses that light songs of this kind hid the brutality of an occupation carried out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a> and with conduct contrary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Convention. An early line recalling the Italian defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896, which the regime did not want brought to mind, was replaced by a more general one.</p><p>The regime itself was embarrassed by the song. During the occupation Ethiopian women lived with Italian men in the concubinage known as madamismo, but from May 1936 the government passed laws forbidding cohabitation and marriage between Italians and the colonised peoples of the empire, which culminated in the racial laws of 1938. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> is said to have hated the song because it seemed to favour the mixing of races, and tried to have it banned; it had become too popular, so he settled for censoring it, and a verse was cut. The authorities also removed picture postcards of Ethiopian women from shop windows in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. With &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovinezza">Giovinezza</a>&quot;, it remained the best-known song of the Fascist years.</p><p>The comic actor Gustavo Cacini later won a plagiarism case against Ruccione, as the refrain drew too closely on one of his own songs, and he received a share of the royalties; many publishers and composers claimed the song in its countless printed editions. Other writers answered it with songs called &quot;Faccetta bianca&quot; (&quot;Little white face&quot;), without its success. In 1938 a Florentine café owner, Enrico Mariotti, named a cocktail after it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s visit. It has been heard in films as a sign of the period: The Best of Enemies (I due nemici, 1961, Guy Hamilton), Martin Scorsese's Mean Streets (1973), Francesco Rosi's Christ Stopped at Eboli, where Blackshirts prepare to leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, Carlos Saura's ¡Ay, Carmela! (1990) and Checco Zalone's Tolo Tolo (2020), where the hero breaks into it in his &quot;attacks of fascism&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A colonial marching song that presents the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> as the liberation of an enslaved Ethiopian girl; racist propaganda in a light, jaunty tone.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="1039836" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Carlo-buti-faccetta-nera-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
